  "extraction_index_sha256": "2737380bb88b4ac609736fefc496e3ad1a98c1ecc72d434c7011b33b0622ad13",
  "judgment": {
    "adequacy": {
      "category": "qualified",
      "excerpt": {
        "end_line": 15,
        "quote": "**Scientific adequacy: qualified.** The group-stage analysis is well aligned with the task: it uses all 54 supplied participant effect maps, checks their recorded hashes, finite values and common geometry, performs an equal-weight one-sample directional group test, and controls positive whole-brain inference using a participant-wise sign-flip max-*t* distribution. Its vmPFC proxy is independently defined from Harvard\u2013Oxford Frontal Medial and Subcallosal labels; the report distinguishes that proxy from a unique anatomical boundary. It reports effect scale limitations, missing first-level variance maps, uncertainty, and the distinction between no evidence and evidence of exact zero. The described ROI estimate is near zero (mean 0.0062, 95% CI \u22120.0204 to 0.0327), with zero ROI voxels surviving the reported whole-brain correction. These methods and limits make the reported absence of corrected positive vmPFC evidence plausible and appropriately narrow.",
        "start_line": 15
      },
      "extraction_status": "explicit"
    },
    "conclusion": {
      "end_line": 23,
      "quote": "In the 54-person NARPS equal-indifference group, the supplied gain-effect maps do **not support a positive vmPFC response** at the group-analysis stage. The pre-defined vmPFC proxy had a near-zero average coefficient, its confidence interval spanned zero, and no voxel in it survived whole-brain family-wise error correction. This is a lack of evidence for the predicted group effect, not evidence that the true effect is exactly zero.",
      "start_line": 23
    },
